Let the vendor Ethernet driver's interfaces be made un-openable.

WHY THIS EXISTS.  `R6-4`'s DoD asks for `ping` in both directions "with my
driver bound and the vendor's **not** loaded".  Three seatings have met the
substance -- a non-shared `request_irq(12)` succeeded, `CPUICR` read
`00000000` until my driver wrote it, and the rings sit at addresses my driver
allocated -- and none has met its words, because the vendor driver is in the
image.  All three are statements about things that HAPPEN NOT TO HAVE
OCCURRED.  This patch makes one of them impossible instead, which is a
different kind of claim and the only one of the three that a single command
can test.

REMOVING THE VENDOR DRIVER OUTRIGHT DOES NOT BUILD, and that is measured.
`CONFIG_RTL_819X_SWCORE=n` leaves ten undefined symbols at the vmlinux link in
four independent places: `bsp_swcore_init` (called from
`boards/rtl8196e/bsp/setup.c:168` under `CONFIG_RTL_819X`, defined at
`AsicDriver/96E/rtl865x_asicBasic.S:390` under `CONFIG_RTL_819X_SWCORE` -- the
two gates come apart), three reached by `jal` from `net/rtl/fastpath/96E/*.S`
which are compiler output carrying no cpp conditionals at all, five from
`drivers/net/wireless/rtl8192cd/`, and `rtl865x_curOpMode` from
`net/rtl/features/rtl_features.c:1097`.  Past the link there is the VLAN
table, which is not a register write at all: `rtl865x_addVlan` ->
`_rtl8651_forceAddAsicEntry(TYPE_VLAN_TABLE, ...)` ->
`96E/rtl865x_asicBasic.S:900`, assembly inside the directory that would
vanish.  That is a GATE, and it is named as one.

THE FIRST DESIGN OF THIS PATCH WAS WRONG AND WAS ABANDONED BEFORE A BUILD.
It suppressed the `eth*` REGISTRATION loop instead, on the reasoning that this
removes the vendor's datapath while keeping its initialisation.  Two readings
killed it, and both are recorded because the second is the expensive kind:

  1. The four-line anchor `if (IF_ETHER!=vlanconfig[i].if_type) { continue; }`
     occurs TWICE in `rtl_nic.c` (`:3786` and `:6403`) with identical
     indentation, so `patch` could not seat the hunk.  A generator that
     checks its anchor is unique is what reported it.

  2. `re865x_probe` ITSELF dereferences the array the loop fills:
     `rtl_nic.c:6615-6616` do
         ((struct dev_priv*)((_rtl86xx_dev.dev[0])->priv))->dev_next = ...
     unconditionally, and `one_sec_timer` at `:4071` derefs `dev[i]` every
     second.  With the loop skipped both are NULL.  The image would have
     oopsed inside probe, on a board with no spare and a loader DMA engine
     still running.  Guarding all of it would have meant patching four to
     eight further vendor sites -- the multi-variable change this ladder
     exists to avoid.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES INSTEAD.  `re865x_open()` at `rtl_nic.c:4174` is the one
place the vendor takes the CPU port: `rtl865x_init_hw()`, the non-shared
`request_irq(dev->irq, ...)`, and the `rtl865x_start()` that writes
`CPUICR = 0xC4000000` are all below its first statement.  The guard returns
`-ENODEV` before any of them.  Every pointer the driver keeps stays valid, no
initialisation is skipped, and no register write changes.

PREDICTION, WRITTEN BEFORE THE BUILD.  With the symbol `n`: `ifconfig eth0 up`
fails and prints an error, and `/proc/net/dev` still LISTS `eth0..eth4`
(they are registered; they cannot be opened).  `ping` through `rlx0` still
completes 4/4 in both directions.  `RLXFW-N1` still reads `00000000` on every
boot -- the vendor's probe still disarms the loader's engine, because probe is
untouched.  My driver's counters are unchanged.  REFUTED BY: `ifconfig eth0
up` succeeding, a boot that does not reach a shell, `RLXFW-N1` reading
anything but `00000000`, or `ping` failing where the control arm succeeds.

WHAT IS GIVEN UP, WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER.  `NET-54`'s
control -- `ifconfig rlx0 down` then `ifconfig eth4 up`, handing the hardware
to the vendor on the same boot -- is unavailable with this symbol `n`.  Any
seating that needs that contrast must boot the control arm, which is one delta
row away.

THE SYMBOL IS `default y`, SO THIS PATCH IS INERT UNTIL THE DELTA ROW TURNS IT
OFF.  That is what makes the control image free.

--- a/arch/rlx/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/rlx/Kconfig
@@ -18,6 +18,22 @@
     bool
     default y
 
+config RLXFW_VENDOR_ETH_OPEN
+    bool "Allow the vendor Ethernet driver's interfaces to be brought up"
+    default y
+    help
+      Say N to make the vendor driver's re865x_open() refuse with -ENODEV.
+      Its eth* interfaces still exist and every hardware initialisation it
+      performs still runs; what becomes impossible is handing the CPU port's
+      DMA engine to it.
+
+      This exists so that "the vendor is not carrying the traffic" can be a
+      structural fact with a positive test -- `ifconfig eth0 up` returns an
+      error -- rather than an inference from three things that happen not to
+      have occurred.
+
+      If unsure, say Y.
+
 config EMBEDDED
     bool
     default y
--- a/drivers/net/rtl819x/rtl_nic.c
+++ b/drivers/net/rtl819x/rtl_nic.c
@@ -4179,6 +4179,16 @@
 	#if defined(CONFIG_RTL_MULTIPLE_WAN)
 	char drvNetif_name[MAX_IFNAMESIZE];
 	#endif
+#ifndef CONFIG_RLXFW_VENDOR_ETH_OPEN
+	/* rlxfw R6-4 rung 1.  Refuse before anything is touched.  This is the
+	 * ONE place the vendor takes the CPU port: rtl865x_init_hw() and the
+	 * rtl865x_start() that writes CPUICR = 0xC4000000 are both below this
+	 * line, and its request_irq() is not shared.  Returning here leaves
+	 * every pointer the driver keeps intact -- which is why the guard is
+	 * here and not on register_netdev().
+	 * config/host-compat/0007 carries the whole reason. */
+	return -ENODEV;
+#endif
 
 	cp = dev->priv;
 	if (cp->opened)
